  <entry>
    <id>https://www.strangerfoods.org/recipes/sticky-salmon-fillet-in-a-ginger-soy-glaze</id>
    <title>Sticky salmon fillet in a ginger soy glaze</title>
    <updated>2026-10-25T17:25:15.252135+00:00</updated>
    <content type="html">&lt;h1 style="text-align:center;font-family:sans-serif;"&gt;Sticky salmon fillet in a ginger soy glaze&lt;/h1&gt;
        &lt;br&gt;
        &lt;img src="https://www.strangerfoods.org/images/sticky-salmon-fillet-in-a-ginger-soy-glaze.webp" style="text-align:center;"/&gt;
        &lt;br&gt;
        &lt;p style="font-family:sans-serif;&gt;When it comes to food, I’m a huge fan of anything with ‘sticky’ in the title and I wanted to create that wonderful quality here, but in a slightly more subtle way, so that it could be enjoyed with a delicately flavoured ingredient like fish. This recipe combines my love for sticky sauce with wonderful salmon; hopefully you enjoy the combination as much as I do.&lt;/p&gt;</content>
    <link href="https://www.strangerfoods.org/recipes/sticky-salmon-fillet-in-a-ginger-soy-glaze"/>
    <published>2026-10-13T00:00:00+00:00</published>
    <media:group>
      <media:thumbnail url="https://www.strangerfoods.org/images/sticky-salmon-fillet-in-a-ginger-soy-glaze.webp" height="288" width="512" time="0"/>
    </media:group>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.strangerfoods.org/recipes/pork-and-purple-broccoli-udon</id>
    <title>Pork and purple broccoli udon</title>
    <updated>2026-10-25T17:25:15.251945+00:00</updated>
    <content type="html">&lt;h1 style="text-align:center;font-family:sans-serif;"&gt;Pork and purple broccoli udon&lt;/h1&gt;
        &lt;br&gt;
        &lt;img src="https://www.strangerfoods.org/images/pork-and-purple-broccoli-udon.webp" style="text-align:center;"/&gt;
        &lt;br&gt;
        &lt;p style="font-family:sans-serif;&gt;I have a special love for rice and noodles, all the types you can think of, and one of my favourites is bánh canh noodles. These are quite difficult to source in the UK, but fortunately udon, which is readily available, has a very similar taste and chewy texture; like bánh canh, it is delicious and super-easy to cook with. This versatile recipe is very quick to put together, and takes full advantage of that amazing udon texture by making sure the sauce clings on to every strand!&lt;/p&gt;</content>
    <link href="https://www.strangerfoods.org/recipes/pork-and-purple-broccoli-udon"/>
    <published>2026-10-17T00:00:00+00:00</published>
    <media:group>
      <media:thumbnail url="https://www.strangerfoods.org/images/pork-and-purple-broccoli-udon.webp" height="288" width="512" time="0"/>
    </media:group>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.strangerfoods.org/recipes/steamed-cod-fillet-with-prawns</id>
    <title>Steamed cod fillet with prawns</title>
    <updated>2026-10-25T17:25:15.251742+00:00</updated>
    <content type="html">&lt;h1 style="text-align:center;font-family:sans-serif;"&gt;Steamed cod fillet with prawns&lt;/h1&gt;
        &lt;br&gt;
        &lt;img src="https://www.strangerfoods.org/images/steamed-cod-fillet-with-prawns.webp" style="text-align:center;"/&gt;
        &lt;br&gt;
        &lt;p style="font-family:sans-serif;&gt;Like many of the best dishes, this recipe came about by chance. I had leftover minced prawns (shrimp) in the fridge from making soup, so I threw them in with the fish I was steaming for lunch and this was the result. Tasty and quick, this is a real winner.&lt;/p&gt;</content>
    <link href="https://www.strangerfoods.org/recipes/steamed-cod-fillet-with-prawns"/>
